      <Comment source="O-27, pujar">Physiological studies have shown that plants have two types of ammonium transporters, optimized for high and low affinity transporters (named HAT and LAT, respectively) |CITS: [12232018]|. The expression of these tansporters in shoots and roots indicate that they could be involved in intracellular transport within the plant in addition to transporting |FRAME: AMMONIUM ammonium| from the soil across the root plasma membrane |CITS: [14701935]|. The transporters are transmembrane proteins, and belong to the ammonium transporter/methylamine permease/rhesus (AMT/MEP/Rh) protein family, which is present in all organisms |CITS: [17026539]|. A cDNA clone of the |FRAME: G-10767 AMT2.2| gene was isolated from |FRAME: TAX-4530 &lt;i&gt;Oryza sativa&lt;/i&gt;| leaf blades. The cDNA was expressed in an ammonium-uptake-defective yeast mutant, restoring ammonium uptake and confirming that it encodes a functional ammonium transporter. The gene is constitutively expressed in both roots and shoots of the |FRAME: TAX-4530 &lt;i&gt;Oryza sativa&lt;/i&gt;| plants, irrespective of the supply of inorganic nitrogen supply |CITS: [12610225]|.</Comment>
